course: money
lesson: 10
title: Regret, silly buys and peer pressure
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Jack spends his whole £10 on the fidget toy everyone has, and two days later nobody cares about fidget toys any more: that is £5 for each day of fun, and a short craze like that is called a fad.
explain: Peer pressure is feeling pushed to buy or do something because friends do; "everyone has them" often means 3 children in a class of 30, which is 1 in 10, not everyone.
explain: Many videos of kids unboxing heaps of toys are really adverts: the toys were given free so that you would want them, and comparing yourself with them, or with older and richer people, can make you unhappy for no reason.
explain: Regret is a feeling that teaches you; a good rule is to "sleep on it", waiting a day and asking "Will I still want this in a month?", and true friends never make you feel bad about money.
fact: A fad is something that is popular for only a short time.
fact: Regret is the feeling of wishing you had not done something; it can help you choose better next time.
fact: Peer pressure is feeling pushed by people your age to buy or do something to fit in.
fact: "Everyone has one" is often not true; counting usually shows it is just a few people.
fact: Mystery bags make buying exciting, but you pay for the surprise and often get things you do not want.
fact: Saying no to spending is okay; good friends do not make you feel bad about money.
fact: Very cheap things sometimes break fast, so you pay twice; paying a bit more for something that lasts can save money.
fact: When someone promises you something too good to be true, like double money back tomorrow, it is probably a trick; tell a trusted adult.
fact: Many shops will not take back something just because you changed your mind, so think before you buy.
fact: Many online videos are sponsored: the maker gets things free or is paid to show them.
fact: Making something yourself can be cheaper and more special than buying it.
fact: "Tight" is British slang for not wanting to spend money; "rip-off" means something that costs far more than it is worth.
exercise: Jack spent £10 on a fidget toy and played with it for 2 days. How much was each day of fun? | £5 | £2 | £10 | £20
exercise: What is a fad? | Something popular for only a short time | Something that lasts for ever and never gets old | A type of mystery bag | A shop's special offer
exercise: In a class of 30, 3 children have the new trainers. What fraction is that? | 1 in 10 | 1 in 3 | 3 in 10 | Everyone
exercise: What is peer pressure? | Feeling pushed by people your age to fit in | A rule your teacher makes for the whole class | The pressure of the air pumped into a car tyre | Saving money together with a friend each week
exercise: Lily paid £5 for a mystery bag with 5 tiny toys inside. How much was each toy? | £1 | 50p | £5 | £2
exercise: What are you really paying for with a mystery bag? | The surprise, not knowing what is inside | Toys that are always rare and worth a lot | A guaranteed favourite toy in every single bag | A bag that can always be taken back to the shop
exercise: Dev has £7. The cinema ticket is £6. How much is left for snacks? | £1 | £6 | £13 | £2
exercise: Big popcorn costs £5. How much more money would Dev need for it after his ticket? | £4 | £5 | £1 | £6
exercise: Harper gets £4 a week. How many weeks to save for an £800 phone? | 200 | 80 | 400 | 20
exercise: Finn bought a £3 car that broke, then a good £7 one. How much did he spend in total? | £10 | £7 | £4 | £3
exercise: What does "buy cheap, buy twice" mean? | Very cheap things may break, so you pay again | Always buy two of everything cheap, just in case | Cheap shops always give you two of everything you buy | Expensive things always break first, so buy cheap
exercise: An older kid says "Lend us your dinner money, I'll pay you back double." What should Tia do? | Say no and tell her teacher | Lend it, double is a great deal | Lend half to be safe | Ask for triple instead
exercise: Molly's £12 toy can't be returned. She gets £4 a week. How many weeks of pocket money did it cost? | 3 | 4 | 12 | 8
exercise: A TikTok kid unboxes 10 toys worth £20 each. What were they worth together? | £200 | £30 | £120 | £2,000
exercise: Why do people in unboxing videos often get toys free? | Companies want the video as an advert | The toys are broken ones | They won them in a competition every day | Shops must give away toys by law
exercise: Thread for 10 bracelets costs £2. How much does each homemade bracelet cost? | 20p | £2 | 10p | £1
exercise: What does it mean to "sleep on it"? | Wait until the next day before deciding | Buy it now and keep it safe under your pillow | Decide while you are tired | Ask the shop for a bed
test: Lily fumes "that's a total rip-off!" about 3 toys she doesn't want for £5. What's the lesson? | With mystery bags you pay for the surprise, so think first | Next time buy two mystery bags | Mystery bags are always worth it | Complain until the shop gives £10 back
test: A mate at the cinema goes "just buy the popcorn, don't be tight." ("Tight" means stingy.) Dev only has £1 left. What's fine to say? | "I'm good, I'll just enjoy the film" | "Lend me £4 and I'll pay you double next week" | "Fine, I'll just skip my bus fare home tonight" | "Popcorn is a need, so I must buy some"
test: "Everyone has the new trainers, I'll be a total loser without them!" Mum counts: 3 out of 30. What's the best reply? | It's 1 in 10, not everyone, and shoes don't make you a loser | She's right, 3 is nearly everyone | Buy two pairs to be extra safe | Ask to change classes
test: Ryan: "why can't I have all that?" after an unboxing video. His dad, a Gen X guy, says "bummer, but it's an ad, mate." ("Bummer" means a disappointment.) What's the point? | The kid got the toys free to advertise them | Ryan's family must be doing something wrong with money | Unboxing kids pay for all their toys | Every kid gets that many toys
test: Finn's grandad: "buy cheap, buy twice, lad." Finn spent £3 + £7. How much would he have saved by buying the £7 car first? | £3 | £7 | £10 | £4
test: Older kid in the playground: "lend us a quid, I'll give you two back tomorrow, deffo." ("Deffo" means definitely.) What's most likely? | You won't see it again; say no and tell a grown-up | You'll get £2 back for sure | You'll get £1.50 back | He'll pay you in sweets instead
test: Ana makes her own bracelets for 20p each while shop ones are £6. How much cheaper is each one? | £5.80 | £6.20 | £5.20 | £6.00
test: What would happen if Jack waited a month before buying the fidget toy? | He'd probably see it was a fad and keep his £10 | The toy would cost £20 by then | He'd have to buy two of them | Nothing would change at all
test: Harper wants her cousin's £800 phone. "She's 16, you're 9." At £4 a week, about how many years would saving take? | Almost 4 years | About 1 year | About 10 years | About 6 months
test: Molly cries "I don't even like it." The toy was £12 and the shop won't take it back. What's a good rule for next time? | Wait a day before buying, and check the return rules | Always buy the most expensive toy | Never buy anything ever again | Buy two so one can go back
test: A London kid says "the bracelets are peng, I want one bare bad." ("Peng" means really nice; "bare" means very.) What does she want? | A bracelet, a lot | Nothing, she hates them | Exactly two bracelets | A full refund from the shop
test: Jack's £10 fidget toy lasted 2 days; Ana's £2 of thread made 10 bracelets that friends loved for months. Which was better value, and why? | Ana's, because she got lots of lasting use from little money | Jack's, because it cost more so it must be better | Both the same, money is money | Jack's, because fads always come back
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises "peer pressure"? | The sense of compulsion to act or purchase in conformity with persons of similar age | A regulation imposed by a school upon its pupils | The pressure exerted by air within a tyre | An arrangement for joint saving among friends
exam: It is given that the sum of ten pounds is expended upon an article enjoyed for two days only. Determine the cost per day of enjoyment. | Five pounds | Two pounds | Ten pounds | Twenty pounds
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with prudence where a person offers to repay double a sum lent to him on the following day. | Declining the offer and informing a trusted adult | Lending the entire sum forthwith upon the promise of double | Lending one half of the sum as a precaution | Demanding a promise of triple repayment
exam: It is given that an inexpensive article costing three pounds fails and is replaced by a durable article costing seven pounds. Ascertain the total expenditure incurred. | Ten pounds | Seven pounds | Four pounds | Three pounds
